  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nThe pressure gradient will continue to weaken through tonight as the \nsurface low over western SD/NE and associated through over western \nSD moves east across our forecast area. This will set the stage for \nanother night of fog. Patchy fog over northeastern South Dakota and \nwest central Minnesota will expand to south central SD tonight into \nSunday morning, with visibility down to 2 miles or less. The trough \nwill be across southeastern SD into west central MN at 12Z, with a \ncold front stretching from a deep low over central Canada through \nnorthwestern MN and bisecting SD northeast to southwest. \n\nThe main impact to this cold front sinking to our south during the \nday Sunday will be the exit of some of the thicker low level \nmoisture. There is low confidence in peaks of sunshine that many \npeople haven't been seen in around a week across northeastern SD and \nwest central MN. We'll see where a band of thicker clouds remains \nSunday (over central or eastern SD).\n\nTaking a look 500mb flow, steady flow out of the southwest will \ndominate our weather pattern late Sunday through at least Tuesday of \nnext week. This will help to bring rain to central SD Sunday night \ninto Monday that will move across northeastern Monday night before \nexiting east during the day Tuesday. A few rumbles of thunder will \naccompany these showers. The WPC excessive rainfall outlook for this \nperiod is in the marginal range, with a 50-80% chance of 0.75\" or \nmore rain over north central SD, mainly north and west of a line \nfrom Redfield to Britton.\n\nDry weather will return Tuesday through the rest of the forecast \nperiod. While high temperatures are forecast to be in the upper 60s \nto mid 70s through Wednesday, they will drop mainly into the 60s \nThursday through Saturday. Lows should remain in the 50s through \nThursday morning, with 40s Friday and Saturday morning.",
